{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>A fast flag can still shift layout — the fix is coupling, not speed</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Even a 50ms flag fetch can cause a visible shift if the browser has already painted and laid out the page before it resolves. Speeding the fetch up reduces the odds a reader notices, it doesn't remove the underlying mechanism, only removing the gap between paint and flag resolution does that.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Reserving space, a skeleton or placeholder sized to match the eventual banner, is a legitimate middle-ground fix when server-side evaluation genuinely isn't available. It prevents the shift itself even though content still swaps in inside that reserved box once the flag resolves.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Worth distinguishing two kinds of flags here: bucketing flags, deciding which variant a given user sees, are usually stable enough to resolve server-side, often from a hashed user ID, with no live network call needed at all. Killswitch flags, meant to instantly disable a broken feature without a deploy, genuinely need to stay pollable and fresh, so the fix there isn't "move it server-side," it's designing the flagged feature as additive UI that only ever appears, never replaces default content the reader has already started engaging with, so there's nothing to visibly swap out from under them.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

